ghpull:810 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "sparql_endpoint: fix CLEAR/DROP running before buffered INSERT/DELETE in the same request (#782)" ;
    bl:number 810 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:782 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr810 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:782 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-11-15T19:10:57Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-15T19:10:57Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr810Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #782 was noticed while fixing #591/#774: apply_prepared_update_with_options buffers InsertData/DeleteData/PatternUpdate across a whole SPARQL Update request into pending_inserts/pending_deletes, applying the net delta only once at the end of the op loop, while CLEAR/DROP apply eagerly during the loop (via clear_graphs_through_reasoner, added in #774). This meant a single request like 'INSERT DATA { x } ; CLEAR DEFAULT' left x present at the end: the eager CLEAR ran while the insert was still only buffered (so it saw an empty default graph), and the buffered insert was applied only after the loop finished, with nothing left afterward to remove it -- backwards from SPARQL 1.1 Update's sequential-operation semantics (section 3.1.3, 'each update operation ... is evaluated in turn'). This direction was explicitly unverified in the issue text (no test had actually run it), so the first step was writing a regression test to confirm the bug was real before designing a fix, rather than assuming the issue's own description was already proven." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:810 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr810Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr810 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr810Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Added flush_pending_delta, called immediately before each eager CLEAR/DROP op (so it sees every buffered op that textually precedes it already applied) and once more after the loop finishes for whatever remains buffered. A request with no CLEAR/DROP in it flushes exactly once, at the end, identical to the pre-fix behavior -- only a request that actually mixes CLEAR/DROP with buffered ops pays for extra flushes (and one extra reasoner call per flush rather than one for the whole request). Four regression tests cover all four orderings worth distinguishing: INSERT;CLEAR (absent -- the bug), CLEAR;INSERT (present -- #774's existing case, control), INSERT;CLEAR;INSERT (present), CLEAR;INSERT;CLEAR (absent) -- true sequential semantics, not just the two single-CLEAR orderings the issue text named." ;
    agp:alternative "The naive fix -- moving CLEAR/DROP into the pending_deletes buffer so it participates in the same end-of-loop net-delta set arithmetic as other ops -- was considered and rejected before writing any code, per #774's own provenance summary (provenance/summaries/pr-774.ttl, agp:Decision.agp:alternative): that PR explicitly rejected this exact restructuring because 'CLEAR DEFAULT ; INSERT DATA { same triple }' must still end with the triple inserted, which batching CLEAR into pending_deletes would get wrong (it would cancel the reinsert against the buffered clear instead of the clear preceding it). The flush-before-each-eager-op scheme satisfies both orderings because it is purely about applying ops in true textual order, never a competing cancellation in the same net-delta arithmetic -- confirmed by the control test (clear_default_then_insert_leaves_triple_present) passing unchanged." .
